task: dataflow-numbers
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-numbers
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfn1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 7

why: the engine has been measured against ITSELF and against a
  hand-written lane in one JVM (1.14x of §20's eight-thread lane).
  Nothing has ever measured what the DISTRIBUTED road costs: the
  coordinator's two round trips, the CBOR partials, four operating-
  system processes instead of eight fibres. Claim 1 of the spec — the
  shuffle is optional and the plan can tell — is an argument about
  what crosses a wire, and no wire has ever been weighed.

what: the Wroclaw job as a `Job[P, R]` a worker process can build
  from its parameters, and a §20 lane that runs it three ways on one
  machine — eight fibres in this JVM, eight partitions over sockets
  in this JVM, eight partitions over four real OS processes — with
  the eleven checksums asserted equal before anything is timed, and
  the BYTES that cross counted rather than described.

what this is NOT, and the boundary must be in the section itself: it
  is not okay-on-a-cluster against Flink-on-a-cluster. §20's Flink is
  a MiniCluster and its Spark is `local[4]`; both are one JVM, and a
  real cluster number for either would need a real cluster. What the
  new rows compare is our own in-process road against our own
  distributed road on the same box, plus what each engine's fixed
  cost buys — and every row says which of those it is.

predict: the wire is cheap for THIS job and the spec's own numbers
  say why before the measurement does (122,679 accumulators reach the
  coordinator where 1.7 million panes would), so the distributed lane
  should land within a small multiple of the in-process one and the
  bytes should be a few megabytes rather than a few hundred. The risk
  is elsewhere: a worker process must BUILD the feed to take its
  slice, and if that is charged to the engine the row measures the
  GTFS parser. It has to be paid once per process and named.
